WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.960
An adult's plasma at rest holds only about 40 nanomoles of free hydrogen ions per

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.400
litre. Metabolism adds acid every day, yet pH stays near 7.4.

3
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.680
What holds it there?

4
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:20.747
Picture pH as the reading of a fraction.

5
00:00:22.027 --> 00:00:25.807
On top, bicarbonate, the base: 24 millimoles per litre.

6
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:30.887
Below, dissolved carbon dioxide, the acid: 1.2.

7
00:00:32.087 --> 00:00:33.247
That is 20 to 1.

8
00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:39.187
In human plasma, pH is 6.1 plus the log of that ratio.

9
00:00:40.747 --> 00:00:44.387
Log 20 is 1.3, so pH is 7.4.

10
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.410
Double both terms, and pH does not move.

11
00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.990
Halve the top alone, and pH falls to 7.1.

12
00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.770
pH follows the ratio, not either number.

13
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.880
A puzzle: the pair's pK is 6.1, well below 7.4.

14
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.740
Seal bicarbonate in a bottle and add acid.

15
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.400
Each proton turns a bicarbonate into carbon dioxide.

16
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.840
The small bottom term grows fast, and pH crashes.

17
00:01:16.983 --> 00:01:19.023
Closed, this would be a poor buffer.

18
00:01:20.263 --> 00:01:24.383
So how can the same pair be the main buffer of the fluid outside your cells?

19
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.090
Because the body is open.

20
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.770
The lungs breathe the new carbon dioxide away.

21
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.070
So the bottom term stays put, and only the top shrinks.

22
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:44.693
Measure buffering as acid needed per unit fall in pH.

23
00:01:46.053 --> 00:01:49.693
Open, the pair manages about 55 millimoles per litre.

24
00:01:51.113 --> 00:01:53.773
Sealed, about 2.6: roughly twentyfold.

25
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:56.387
Here is the one idea.

26
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:02.267
pH follows one ratio, bicarbonate over dissolved carbon dioxide.

27
00:02:03.647 --> 00:02:06.267
The lungs hold the bottom, starting within minutes.

28
00:02:07.507 --> 00:02:09.447
The kidney sets the top, over days.

29
00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:12.427
But buffering is not removal.

30
00:02:13.827 --> 00:02:18.227
Sulfuric acid from sulfur-containing amino acids cannot be breathed off.

31
00:02:19.527 --> 00:02:21.047
Its protons stay in the body.

32
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:25.727
The bicarbonate they spent is gone until the kidney makes more.

33
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.760
Human kidneys have two jobs.

34
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:35.300
They filter about 4,000 millimoles of bicarbonate a day, roughly eighty times the

35
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:41.800
day's acid. Job one is to take it back: one vanishes from the tubule, one appears in

36
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.640
the blood. No new base is made.

37
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.470
Job two repays the debt.

38
00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.430
Making new bicarbonate, by excreting acid, is the only way.

39
00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:55.630
Some acid leaves riding on phosphate.

40
00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.530
Most leaves as ammonium, made from glutamine.

41
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.380
So the ratio holds, and an ordinary pair guards the blood.

42
00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.800
Breathing clears the new carbon dioxide within minutes, and the kidney repays the

43
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.040
spent base over days.
